# 3.2 EasyEDA Pro PCB foundation

Read this reference when the PCB foundation from [3.1 PCB design and review](../../3.1-pcb-review.md) must be created or changed in EasyEDA Pro: layers, outline, functional keepouts, and reference copper.

This flow owns only the physical foundation. 3.1 owns placement, routing, and net rules. [3.3 PCB return paths and grounding](3.3-pcb-grounding.md) owns return and ground vias.

Use this flow twice when needed:

```text
First pass: read the PCB → layers and outline → functional keepouts
After placement and critical routing: create or repour reference copper → check and save
```

## Confirm inputs

Before writing, confirm:

- current project and target PCB document;
- outline, mounting holes, and mechanical interfaces;
- selected copper-layer count and role of each layer;
- reference layers for important signals;
- functional keepouts to implement;
- plane and pour nets to create or preserve;
- existing placement, routing, vias, and copper that must remain;
- current fabricator capability or explicit stage assumptions.

Each applicable item may be confirmed, provisional, or not applicable. Pause only when a gap prevents the current edit or risks overwriting important existing content.

## Use existing Actions

Relevant Actions include:

- `pcb-layer-stack`: read and change the logical copper-layer structure;
- `pcb-component-geometry`: read component, pad, and package geometry;
- `pcb-functional-keepouts`: read and change purpose-specific keepouts;
- `pcb-ground-pours`: read and change bounded reference copper.

Actual support comes from the current manifest, capability check, and execution result. A local operation outside existing Action coverage may use a current EasyEDA method if its result is read back.

## 1. Read the current PCB

Read objects relevant to this task:

- outline and slots;
- copper layers and order;
- components, pads, and mounting holes;
- existing tracks, vias, and planes;
- keepouts and other Regions;
- current save state.

Canvas appearance helps interpretation but does not replace object identity, layer, net, or geometry.

When manual placement or routing already exists, identify the objects affected by this change. Do not regenerate the whole foundation over existing work.

## 2. Establish layers and outline

Implement the 3.1 decision:

- copper count and order;
- main role of each layer;
- reference layers and plane nets;
- outline, slots, and required mechanical boundaries;
- mounting holes and edge-interface locations.

Layer naming or plane-net configuration does not prove that copper exists. Dielectric thickness, copper weight, and impedance parameters still come from the fabricator or project assumption.

Adding a layer is often local. Removing a layer, changing the role of an occupied layer, or moving an outline that already contains copper and components may destroy content. When such a real conflict exists, identify affected objects and the intended treatment before continuing.

Read back layer order, outline, and critical mechanical objects after writing.

## 3. Implement functional keepouts

Create keepouts only for a defined engineering purpose, such as:

- antenna clearance;
- microphone, pressure, optical, or airflow opening;
- electrical isolation;
- mounting or moving mechanical space;
- a manufacturer-defined no-copper area;
- an area that restricts parts, tracks, vias, or copper.

For each keepout, define:

- purpose;
- source;
- boundary;
- affected layers and object types;
- any necessary local exception.

Silkscreen outline, courtyard, and visual whitespace are not automatically copper keepouts. Confirm the boundary from the actual component, package, or mechanical geometry.

Read back the Region's layer, rule, and geometry, and check that it does not cover pads, outline, required routes, or other protected objects.

## 4. Create reference copper

Run this section after placement and critical routing when the resulting copper shape and return channels can be checked against real geometry.

After outline and keepouts are clear, create the required plane or pour.

Confirm:

- target net;
- layer;
- pour boundary;
- clearance to edge, holes, pads, and other nets;
- actual keepout effect;
- thermal or direct connection style;
- slots, partitions, and return channels that must remain.

A pour boundary does not prove that copper was generated. Repour and inspect the actual fill, islands, necks, disconnects, and critical clearances.

On a complex board, prove one representative layer or region before expanding. If one geometry cannot be resolved, pause that region rather than blocking unrelated PCB work.

## 5. Check and save

After the foundation increment:

1. Repour affected copper.
2. Read back changed layers, Regions, and pours.
3. Confirm the outline, mechanical objects, and prior content remain.
4. Visually inspect critical keepouts and copper shape.
5. Run configured DRC.
6. Save the PCB.
7. Confirm the saved document retains the result.

DRC covers configured rule geometry only. Whether reference copper provides a good return path still depends on actual placement and routing.

## Failure and recovery

Follow [0.3 EasyEDA Pro Provider](../../0.3-easyeda-pro.md) and read current state again before recovery. PCB-foundation recovery may complete or undo only layers, outline objects, Regions, and pours that this operation can identify precisely.

## Finish the first pass

Before returning to 3.1 for placement and critical routing, confirm:

- layer order and roles match the design;
- outline, mounting holes, and main mechanical interfaces are correct;
- functional keepouts were implemented and read back.

Return to [3.1 PCB design and review](../../3.1-pcb-review.md) for placement and critical routing.

## Finish the copper pass

After placement and critical routing, return here and confirm:

- required reference copper was actually generated;
- critical regions have no obvious islands, disconnects, or wrong clearance;
- the PCB is saved and DRC has no unexplained current issue.

Then read [3.3 PCB return paths and grounding](3.3-pcb-grounding.md).
